#set($h1 = '#')
#set($h2 = '##')
#set($h3 = '###')
$h1 ${SERVER_NAME}

$h2 1. Overview

${SERVER_NAME} is a **native executable** MCP server generated by Database Navigator,
built with Micronaut and compiled ahead-of-time with GraalVM.
It connects to Oracle Database and exposes your SQL queries as MCP tools.

Unlike the Standard Java server, this executable requires **no Java installation to run**.
It starts near-instantly and is specific to the operating system and CPU architecture it was built on
(it will not run on a different platform — rebuild it there instead).

Transport is configured in `mcp-config.yaml` and supports:
- `stdio` (default)
- `http` (streamable HTTP)

---

$h2 2. Running the server

$h3 2.1. STDIO (default)

Run the executable directly; the MCP client starts and owns the process:

```
./${EXECUTABLE_NAME}
```

An optional first argument overrides the configuration file location:

```
./${EXECUTABLE_NAME} /path/to/mcp-config.yaml
```

By default `mcp-config.yaml` is expected next to the executable, along with the `wallet/` directory.

$h3 2.2. HTTP

Set `transport: http` in `mcp-config.yaml`, then start the executable.
The MCP endpoint is served on `http://127.0.0.1:${HTTP_PORT}/mcp`.

After changing `mcp-config.yaml`, restart the executable and reconnect your MCP client.

---

$h2 3. Custom Tool Framework

The server loads datasource and tool definitions from `mcp-config.yaml`.
You can edit this file after generation to adjust SQL, descriptions, and parameters — no rebuild needed.
`dataSource` is required.

A tool entry supports:
- `description`: tool description
- `statement`: SQL statement (read-only query that returns rows)
- `parameters`: optional list of bind parameters

Notes:
- Tool SQL is executed with query semantics (`executeQuery`), so use `SELECT`-style statements that return a result set.
- DML/DDL/anonymous PL/SQL blocks are not supported in this generated server flow.

Parameter fields:
- `name`: bind variable name (without `:`)
- `type`: JSON schema type (`string`, `integer`, `number`, `boolean`)
- `format`: optional metadata for string types (for example `date`)
- `required`: whether this parameter is required

$h3 3.1. Generated tools

#foreach($tool in $TOOLS)
- **${tool.name}** — ${tool.description}
#end

---

$h2 4. Credentials and Security

Database credentials are stored in the `wallet/` directory (Oracle auto-login wallet, `cwallet.sso`).
- Keep the `wallet/` directory private; it is protected only by file-system permissions.
- To use different credentials without the wallet, uncomment `username`/`password` under `dataSource`
  in `mcp-config.yaml`. Values placed there take precedence over the wallet.

---

$h2 5. Rebuilding from source

The `source-project/` directory contains the complete Maven project used to build this executable.

Rebuilding the **native executable** requires:
- A **GraalVM JDK** (17 or newer) as the Maven JVM — `native-image` is invoked during the build.
- A platform C toolchain (Xcode command-line tools on macOS, Visual Studio Build Tools on Windows,
  gcc/glibc-devel on Linux).

```
mvn clean package -Dpackaging=native-image
```

To build a regular runnable JAR instead (works on any JDK 17+, no GraalVM needed):

```
mvn clean package
java -jar target/${SERVER_NAME}-1.0.jar
```

The GraalVM reachability metadata for the Oracle JDBC driver and wallet support lives in
`src/main/resources/META-INF/native-image/` — keep it in sync with the Oracle driver version
declared in `pom.xml`.
